      "summary": [
        {
          "sentence": "Credit: TechCrunch/Flickr(CC BY 4.0) Read the original article Ethereum co-founder Vitalik Buterin has highlighted a major difference between Bitcoin mining costs and artificial intelligence (AI) expenses , revealing how electricity and hardware investments influence profitability in both industries.",
          "sentiment": {
            "score": 0,
            "polarity": "neutral",
            "mentions": null
          }
        },
        {
          "sentence": "In a recent post on X, Buterin explained that Bitcoin mining expenses are divided almost equally between electricity and computing infrastructure.",
          "sentiment": {
            "score": 0,
            "polarity": "neutral",
            "mentions": null
          }
        },
        {
          "sentence": "In contrast, AI inference costs are overwhelmingly driven by hardware investments, creating different economic challenges for the two sectors.",
          "sentiment": {
            "score": 0,
            "polarity": "neutral",
            "mentions": null
          }
        },
        {
          "sentence": "According to a cost breakdown shared by Buterin, the estimatedcost of mining one Bitcoin stands at approximately $93,700.",
          "sentiment": {
            "score": 0.05,
            "polarity": "positive",
            "mentions": null
          }
        },
        {
          "sentence": "Electricity consumed directly by mining equipment accounts for $40,600, representing 43.3% of total expenses.",
